From b1157847ad8b0fa4ddec50c6ccd43274874077ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 17:39:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Capacity: no wrap past the reservation caps; strict account
 decoding [skip ci]

ReserveCapacity's checks added the instruction's units and max_output
to the pool's sums without a range check. permutation-rules is built
without overflow checks in release (only permutation-frontier and
frontier-abi have them), so in a release build a reservation of
u32::MAX copies, or of i64::MAX output, wrapped past the grant's unit
cap, the pool's lent copies and the grant's output budget and was
accepted (debug builds panicked instead). A sum past the type's range
now exceeds the cap it is compared with (GrantUnitsExceeded,
CapacityExceeded, GrantOutputExceeded), the grant's output sum
saturates, and LendCapacity's owner sequence refuses Overflow instead
of wrapping. The reference kernel (workstreams/09-capacity/kernel)
gets the same change, so the rules port stays equal to it.

Decoding: a flag byte that is neither 0 nor 1 (the last owner op's lend
flag, a job's started and refunded flags, an occupancy row's open flag,
a job's lot link), a deposit's input past the recipe's two, or a
grant's unknown recipe is not a record of ours and is refused (it was
read as false, or kept).

Tests (no key generated, derived or signed): the rules' boundary tests,
which failed before (debug: overflow panic; release: Ok where a refusal
is due), pass in debug and release; the layout tests reject the
malformed bytes; a host test through the real dispatch refuses a
u32::MAX or i64::MAX reservation and a claim the vault cannot hold,
with the job, the pool and the vault byte-for-byte unchanged; the
rules port agrees with the kernel over 3,000 random runs.
Undecided rules untouched.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01C5Nj2Y9wne2WBMdMysfzXe
